Recently, I got into a very slight argument with my Mom about social media. I'm not a huge fan of Facebook or Instagram, and I honestly don't like having my own pictures posted on there, because I'm a relatively private person and just keep in touch with people through other means. My Mom is, in my opinion, far too eager to post pictures and updates about just about anything on social media, but I can set aside my own hang ups with it for the sake of being a part of this one thing that she really enjoys doing.

But I draw the line at my own child. I grew up right as social media was growing, and it wasn't a huge thing while I was really young, so I was able to have some agency over when I made my "debut" on the internet so to speak. I want to afford my own child the same privilege, and I don't want anyone posting photos of my kid before they're at least old enough to have a conversation about it and see if it's something they're into. When I told my parents this, they said I was being a little ridiculous, and that I should be eager to show the world my child whenever I have one. They might be right, and I might be being ridiculous, but would I be an \*asshole\* for asking friends and family to not post pictures on social media? I understand there isn't a whole lot of privacy in the modern age, but I want to afford my own children as much as I can give them while they grow to the point that they can make their own decisions on the matter.

